WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.400
Some conventions don't live in a folder. They follow a file type, and those files are scattered over your tree.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:15.000
Path scoped rules handle that case. Loading instructions only when Claude opens a matching file.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:21.912
In this lesson, we'll cover where these rules live, how the paths field scopes them, and when to

4
00:00:21.912 --> 00:00:23.927
reach for one. Let's dig in.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.500
A project rule is nothing more exotic than a markdown file in the projects.claude rules folder.

6
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:37.250
Claude code discovers every markdown file in there, including ones nested inside subfolders, so you

7
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.431
can group them however you like.

8
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:46.500
Paths frontmatter is a small YAML block at the very top of a rule file, and it holds a list of glob patterns.

9
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.500
Conditional loading is what that block buys you.

10
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:56.359
When a rule carries a paths list, it stops loading on every session, and starts loading only when

11
00:00:56.359 --> 00:00:59.439
Claude reads a file matching one of those patterns.

12
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:07.500
Leave the paths list out, and the rule loads at launch, exactly like your project claude.md does.

13
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.500
Setting rules up is deliberately unremarkable.

14
00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:15.500
You make a rules folder inside .claude, and you drop markdown files in it.

15
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:23.000
Each file should cover one topic, with a name that says what it is, like testing.md or apidesign.md.

16
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.828
Discovery walks the folder recursively, so if you'd rather organize into a frontend folder and a

17
00:01:28.828 --> 00:01:31.439
backend folder underneath, that works fine.

18
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.880
A rule with no paths field in its frontmatter behaves the same way your project claude.md does,

19
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.432
loading at the start of every session.

20
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.000
The one-topic-per-file habit is worth keeping.

21
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.000
It makes rules reviewable in a pull request, and easy for a teammate to find and change later.

22
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Now the part that makes rules different.

23
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.500
Add a paths list to the frontmatter, and the rule becomes conditional.

24
00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:03.048
The field takes one or more glob patterns, and you can use brace expansion to cover several

25
00:02:03.048 --> 00:02:04.939
extensions in a single pattern.

26
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Here's the detail worth holding on to.

27
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.000
A path-scoped rule fires when Claude reads a file that matches not on every tool call, and not at startup.

28
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.000
So the rule is absent from context right up until the moment it's relevant, and then it's there.

29
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.000
That read trigger is the entire mechanism.

30
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
If you remember one thing about paths, remember that the match happens against files Claude actually opens.

31
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.500
Let's read these patterns, because glob syntax is where people slow down.

32
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.500
The typescript pattern uses a double star for the directory portion, which means any depth,

33
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.500
so it catches every typescript file in the project, however deeply it's buried.

34
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.064
The source directory pattern anchors to that folder, and then takes everything underneath it,

35
00:02:51.064 --> 00:02:52.440
whatever the extension.

36
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.500
The bare markdown pattern is the one that surprises people.

37
00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:03.500
With no directory part in front of it, it matches the project root only, so nested files fall outside it.

38
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:09.500
The brace expansion pattern carries two extensions at once, instead of you writing two separate lines.

39
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.500
These are ordinary globs.

40
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.500
If you've ever written a git in your file, you can already read them.

41
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.500
It's worth being explicit about why conditional loading is more than tidiness.

42
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:25.500
Everything that loads at launch occupies the same context window your actual work needs,

43
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.500
and adherence drops as that file grows.

44
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:36.500
Once a Claude.md runs past roughly 200 lines, the guidance in it starts getting followed less consistently.

45
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.500
Pathscoped rules attack that directly.

46
00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:43.500
Instead of one file carrying every convention for every part of the code base,

47
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:49.500
the terraform conventions sit quiet until Claude actually opens a terraform file.

48
00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:56.500
The always-on context stays small, and a small always-on context is one the model follows more reliably.

49
00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.500
You're trading breadth at launch for precision at the moment of use.

50
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.500
So, when do you reach for which?

51
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.500
A subdirectory Claude.md scopes-by-folder.

52
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:14.500
The file's location is the scope, which fits a convention that genuinely belongs to one area of the tree,

53
00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.500
like an infrastructure directory that owns its own standards.

54
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:24.500
A pathscoped rule scopes by pattern, and a pattern crosses folders freely.

55
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.500
That's the case a subdirectory file simply can't cover.

56
00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.500
Think about test files.

57
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.500
They're scattered through the code base next to the code they exercise,

58
00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:36.500
so there's no single folder to put the instructions in.

59
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:41.500
A rule matching every test file catches all of them wherever they live.

60
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.500
Convention follows the folder, use a subdirectory file.

61
00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:48.500
Convention follows the file type, use a rule.

62
00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:50.500
Rules aren't only a project-level tool.

63
00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.500
You can put personal rules in the rules folder under your home directory,

64
00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.500
and those apply to every project on your machine.

65
00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:03.500
This is the right home for preferences that are about you, rather than about any one code base.

66
00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.500
Precedence works the way you'd want.

67
00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:09.500
User rules load first, and project rules load after,

68
00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:13.500
which gives the project the stronger position when the two overlap.

69
00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:19.500
The folder also follows symlinks, so a team can keep one shared set of rules in a single place,

70
00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:26.500
and link it into several repositories, rather than copying files around and watching them drift apart.

71
00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:33.500
One more distinction that shows up constantly, because rules and skills look similar from a distance.

72
00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.500
Both hold instructions you've written.

73
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.500
The difference is when they reach the model.

74
00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.500
A rule loads every session or whenever a matching file gets opened,

75
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.500
so it's always shaping behavior in the background.

76
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:51.500
A skill loads on demand, either because you invoked it, or because Claude judged it relevant to what you asked.

77
00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.500
That maps cleanly onto the content.

78
00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:00.500
A standing convention, something Claude should honor whenever it touches this kind of file, is a rule.

79
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.500
A multi-step procedure you run occasionally is a skill.

80
00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.500
Ask which one you're holding, and the choice usually answers itself.

81
00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:12.500
Folder scope and pattern scope aren't the same thing.

82
00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:16.500
A subdirectory file covers the branch of the tree it sits in.

83
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:21.500
A path-scoped rule covers every file matching a glob, wherever it lives.

84
00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:28.132
When a convention follows the file type instead of the location, like tests scattered next to the

85
00:06:28.132 --> 00:06:29.431
code they exercise,

86
00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:34.500
the pattern is the only thing that catches all of them, so keep those things in mind.

87
00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.500
That closes out this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

